WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.310
Now that you've got the files and folder setup that you'll be working with,

00:00:03.310 --> 00:00:06.640
how are you going to actually write the CSS to define your framework?

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:10.940
I suggested using a 12 column grid because it's really easy to create all of

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.980
the columns that you need, but what else do you think you'll need to define?

00:00:13.980 --> 00:00:19.160
Will you need to create a CSS class that includes a row of 100% page width?

00:00:19.160 --> 00:00:22.970
A CSS class for a row of one-twelfth of the page width?

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:25.790
Remember, 12 here is coming from the number of columns.

00:00:25.790 --> 00:00:30.710
Will we only need to create one column class of one-twelfths of the page width,

00:00:30.710 --> 00:00:32.250
to define our columns?

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:36.360
Will we need to create multiple columns of everything between 1 over 12 and

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:38.420
12 over 12 page widths?

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:43.366
That means we'll need to create a column class for 1 over 12 page widths, 1 for

00:00:43.366 --> 00:00:47.236
2 over 12 page widths, 1 for 3 over 12 page widths, 1 for

00:00:47.236 --> 00:00:51.822
4 over 12 page widths and so on, or, will we only need to create columns of 2

00:00:51.822 --> 00:00:57.060
over 12, 3 over 12 and 4 over 12 page widths to create our framework?

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.490
Oh and by the way,

00:00:58.490 --> 00:01:03.300
the reason I put these dots here is because when you're writing CSS you'll need

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.680
to identify that something is a class by putting a dot in front of it.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:08.830
So it's good to get used to that notation now.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:12.420
Think about this question, and when you're done check all that apply.

